[Line "Petrosian System: 7.d5 a5 8.Bg5"]
[Section "Petrosian System: 7.d5"]
[Summary "White closes the centre early and pins the knight to stop ...f5. Black unpins with ...Qe8, forces f3 with ...h5 and activates his bad bishop via h6 and e3."]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. d5 { [%cal Rc1g5] White closes the centre
before the knight has come to c6, so there is no ...Ne7 and no automatic kingside attack. He keeps the
choice of where to castle and intends Bg5, to make ...f5 hard to organise. } 7... a5 { [%cal Gb8a6,Ga6c5]
[%csl Yc5] The c5-square is the natural home for Black's queen's knight in this structure, and ...a5
secures it against b4 before the knight sets out. }
( 7... Nbd7 { The old main line. The knight reaches c5 as well, but it blocks the bishop and takes d7 from
the other knight, and Black usually has to accept 8.Bg5 h6 9.Bh4 g5, with a weakened kingside. } )
( 7... Na6 { A flexible relative of the main move, recommended by Gallagher: Black keeps ...a5 in reserve
and may save it altogether. The plans are the same, and it often transposes. } )
8. Bg5 { [%csl Rf6] Petrosian's idea. With the knight pinned Black cannot play ...Nh5, ...Ne8 or ...Nd7
to free the f-pawn. } 8... h6 { Asking the question while the answer costs White something: on e3 the
bishop is hit by ...Ng4, and on h4 it can later be shut in or trapped. } 9. Bh4
( 9. Be3 Ng4 10. Bd2 f5 { Black has gained ...h6 and ...f5 for nothing and stands well. } )
9... Na6 { Development first. The pin is broken with the queen, not with the g-pawn. }
( 9... g5?! { The old way of breaking the pin, which the main line was invented to avoid. } 10. Bg3 Nh5
11. h4 { White breaks up the pawns before Black has consolidated: after 11...g4 the knight goes to d2
or h2, f5 is a hole that only White can use and Black's bishop on g7 has no future. } )
10. Nd2 { [%cal Rf2f3] The knight makes way for f3 and is ready to meet ...Nc5 with b3, a3 and b4. }
10... Qe8 { [%cal Gf6h7,Gf7f5] The pin is broken without weakening anything. The queen also supports
...Nh7 and ...f5 and looks at a4. } 11. O-O
( 11. a3 { Starting the queenside play before castling. } 11... Bd7 { The same answer as in the main
line: now b4 is not possible, and Rb1 can be met by ...a4. } )
11... Nh7 { [%cal Gf7f5,Gh6h5] Clearing the f-pawn, and from h7 the knight supports ...h5 and can come to
g5. } 12. a3 Bd7 { [%csl Yb4] A precise move. The queen now protects the rook on a8, so 13.b4 loses a pawn
to 13...axb4 14.axb4 Nxb4. White has to spend another move on preparation. } 13. b3 { So that ...a4 can be answered
by b4. }
( 13. Kh1 { White prepares f3 and Bf2 without allowing a check on e3. } 13... a4 { Fixing the queenside:
b4 can now be taken en passant and the knight has c5 for good. } )
13... h5 { [%cal Gg6g5,Gh5h4] The threat is ...g5 followed by ...h4, trapping the bishop, so White must
make room for it. }
( 13... f5 { The sharp alternative, which leads to a famous exchange sacrifice. } 14. exf5 gxf5 15. Bh5
Qc8 16. Be7 Re8 17. Bxe8 Qxe8 18. Bh4 e4 { Yusupov-Kasparov, Barcelona 1989. For the exchange Black has
the long diagonal, e5 and d3 for his knights and a mobile pawn pair; White's rooks have no open file.
Black won, and the sacrifice is still considered sound, but the main line asks less of Black's memory. } )
14. f3 Bh6 { [%cal Gh6e3] [%csl Ye3] The move ...h5 was played for this as well. White's f3 has opened
the diagonal to e3, and the bishop that was shut in behind its own pawns becomes Black's most active
piece. } 15. Rb1 Be3+ 16. Bf2 Bxf2+ { The straightforward choice: the bad bishop is exchanged for White's
good one. }
( 16... Bc5 { Keeping the bishops on is also possible: the bishop is safe on c5 and annoying, and White
needs b4 to shift it. A matter of taste. } )
17. Rxf2 Qe7 { [%cal Gh5h4,Ge7g5,Gf7f5] **Verdict:** balanced. With the dark-squared bishops gone Black's
king is safe and his remaining bishop is the better one. He continues with ...h4, ...Qg5, ...Kg7 and
...Nf6-h5-f4, or with ...f5 when White is busy elsewhere. White will play b4, and after ...axb4, axb4
Black keeps the queenside closed with ...b6 or strikes back with ...c5. Neither side can easily break
through, and the better manoeuvrer wins. } *

[Line "Petrosian System: 8.Be3, 8.O-O and 8.h3"]
[Section "Petrosian System: 7.d5"]
[Summary "White's quieter eighth moves. Black uses ...Ng4 against a bishop on e3 and otherwise develops with ...Na6, heading for the main-line set-up."]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. d5 a5 8. Be3 { The bishop takes c5 away
from the knight and White keeps Nd2 and g4 in mind. It is also a target. }
( 8. O-O { White commits his king, which rules out the plans with g4. } 8... Na6 9. Bg5
( 9. Qc2 { Defending e4 so that the knight can stay on f3. } 9... Nc5 { The knight ties White to the
e4-pawn, and Black follows with ...Bd7 or ...Nh5-f4. } )
9... h6 10. Bh4 Qe8 11. Nd2 { The main line by transposition. } 11... Nh7 )
( 8. h3 { The plan with Be3 and g4, restraining ...f5. } 8... Na6 { This position belongs to the h3
systems and is covered in that chapter; Black's set-up with ...Nc5, ...Bd7 and ...Qe8 or ...Nh5 is
the same. } )
8... Ng4 9. Bg5 f6 10. Bh4 Na6 { Black has been made to play ...f6, which blocks the bishop for the
moment, and in return the pin has gone and the knight on g4 has a good route home. } 11. Nd2 Nh6
{ [%cal Gh6f7,Gg7h6] The knight goes to f7, where it covers g5, e5 and d6 and makes ...Bh6 possible.

**Verdict:** Black completes the set-up with ...Nf7, ...Bd7 and ...Qe8, and then chooses between ...Bh6,
exchanging the bad bishop, and ...f5. White's bishop on h4 is out of play once the f-pawn has moved on,
and his queenside advance needs the same a3, b3, Rb1 and b4 as in the main line. Equal chances. } *

[Line "Gligoric System: 7.Be3 Ng4 8.Bg5 f6 9.Bh4"]
[Section "Gligoric System: 7.Be3"]
[Summary "White delays castling and keeps the centre fluid. Black chases the bishop and, once it is committed to h4, plays ...Nc6 after all, reaching a Mar del Plata with extra features."]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. Be3 { [%cal Rd4d5,Rf3d2] The Gligoric System.
White supports d4, keeps the tension and has not castled, which makes Black's usual attack less
attractive. } 7... Ng4 { The bishop is attacked before h3 can be played. The alternatives 7...exd4,
7...Na6, 7...c6 and Nunn's 7...h6 are all respectable; this is the most direct and the one most
repertoire books choose. }
( 7... Nc6?! { What White is hoping for. } 8. d5 Ne7 9. Nd2 { A Mar del Plata in which White has saved a
move on castling and has the knight on d2 at once. He is ready for b4 and c5, his king can stay in the
centre or go to the queenside, and Black's attack has no target. Theory gives White the better game. } )
8. Bg5 f6 { The bishop is blocked, but only until the pawn moves on to f5. } 9. Bh4 { [%csl Rd8] The
bishop stays on the diagonal of the queen. As long as it is there the f-pawn is pinned in effect, since
...f5 would uncover an attack on d8, so Black has to prepare the advance. } 9... Nc6 { [%cal Rc6d4] Now
the knight comes out after all. The difference from move seven is that after 10.d5 Ne7 White's bishop stands on h4,
outside the pawn chain, where ...g5 will hit it with tempo, and the knight on g4 has a good route home
via h6 and f7. }
( 9... g5 { The other main line, which Vigorito covers alongside 9...Nc6. Black gains space at once and
brings the knight to f7, at the cost of weakening f5 and h5 before White has committed his centre. It
is sound, and a matter of taste. } 10. Bg3 Nh6 )
10. d5
( 10. O-O { Keeping the tension, and transposing to the line 7.O-O Nc6 8.Be3 Ng4 9.Bg5 f6 10.Bh4, where
the continuation is discussed. } 10... g5 11. Bg3 Nh6 )
10... Ne7 11. Nd2 { The knight on g4 is attacked and f3 is prepared. } 11... Nh6 { [%cal Gh6f7] Not a bad
square in this structure: the knight goes to f7, where it defends d6, the base of the chain, and
supports ...g4 later. } 12. f3 { Giving the bishop f2. } 12... g5
( 12... c5 { A positional alternative. Black blocks the queenside before turning to the other wing;
after 13.dxc6 bxc6 he has the b-file and control of d5. It scores well and suits players who dislike
races. } )
13. Bf2 f5 14. c5 Ng6 { [%cal Gg5g4,Gh6f7] **Verdict:** a Mar del Plata race with two differences. Black's
king's knight stands on h6 instead of f6, heading for f7, where it guards d6 and makes the c5 break less
dangerous. And White's king is still on e1: he can castle long, or play g4 to block the kingside, so
Black must be ready to switch to the h-file with ...Nf7 and ...h5. Chances are equal. } *

[Line "Gligoric System: 9.Bc1"]
[Section "Gligoric System: 7.Be3"]
[Summary "The bishop goes home, claiming that ...f6 and the knight on g4 are misplaced. Black develops with ...Nc6 and gets ...f5 in."]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. Be3 Ng4 8. Bg5 f6 9. Bc1 { White has lost
no time, since Black's last two moves are not obviously useful, and h3 is a threat. } 9... Nc6 { The
most natural move now that d5 would not come with Nd2 ready. The older 9...exd4 10.Nxd4 f5 opens the
game at once and is also playable. } 10. O-O { Transposing to the line 7.O-O Nc6 8.Be3 Ng4 9.Bg5 f6
10.Bc1, where Black's play is explained. }
( 10. d5 { Closing the centre with the king still at home. } 10... Ne7 11. h3 Nh6 { [%cal Gh6f7,Gf6f5]
White hopes to use the knight's position with g4 or h4-h5. Black answers with ...Nf7, after which
h5 can be met by ...g5, and then plays ...f5 or ...c5. The knight on f7 is well placed and White's
king has no safe home, so the chances are balanced. } )
10... f5 11. dxe5 Ngxe5 *

[Line "Exchange Variation: 7.dxe5"]
[Section "Exchange Variation: 7.dxe5"]
[Summary "White trades queens and tries to use his lead in development. Black meets the threats to c7 and f6 exactly, plays ...c6 and equalises; the d4-square is his long-term asset."]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. dxe5 dxe5 8. Qxd8 Rxd8 9. Bg5 { [%cal Rc3d5]
[%csl Rf6,Rc7] The only dangerous move. The knight on f6 is pinned against the rook, and Nd5 threatens
both it and c7. }
( 9. Nxe5 { This wins nothing. } 9... Nxe4 { The pawn comes straight back, because the knight on e5 is
attacked by the bishop. } 10. Nxe4
( 10. Nxf7? { Trying to win the exchange. } 10... Bxc3+ { The check gains the decisive tempo. } 11. bxc3
Kxf7 { Black has won a piece. } )
10... Bxe5 { Black has the more active bishops and no problems at all. } )
( 9. Nd5 { The same idea without the pin. } 9... Nxd5 10. cxd5 c6 11. Bc4 cxd5 12. Bxd5 Nd7 { The knight
will challenge the bishop from b6 or f6. Black is equal for the same reasons as in the main line. } )
9... Re8 { [%cal Gb8a6,Gc7c6] The rook leaves the pin, so that Nd5 can be met by ...Nxd5. On e8 it also
guards e5, which frees the bishop and the knights from that duty. } 10. Nd5
( 10. O-O-O { Development with a threat: Nd5 or Nb5 would now hit c7 with the rook on the d-file. }
10... Na6 { The knight covers c7 and is on its way to c5. } 11. Ne1 { The knight heads for d5 by c2 and
e3. } 11... c6 { [%csl Yd4] Taking d5 away for good. Black follows with ...Nc5, ...a5 and ...Ne6-d4, and
it is White who has to be careful: his e4-pawn and the d4-square are permanent weaknesses. } )
10... Nxd5 11. cxd5 c6 { Black challenges the pawn at once, before White can support it with Rc1 or
Nd2-c4. } 12. Bc4 cxd5 13. Bxd5 Nd7 { [%cal Gd7c5,Gd7b6] The knight keeps both b6 and c5 in view and
does not block the c8-bishop for long. The bishop on d5 looks strong, but it will be exchanged or
driven back. } 14. Nd2 { Heading for c4 and d6. } 14... Nc5 15. O-O-O Be6 { [%csl Yd4] **Verdict:** equal.
The strong bishop is exchanged, after 16.Bxe6 Nxe6 the knight attacks g5 and looks at d4 and f4, and
neither side has a weakness the other can reach. In the long run Black's structure is the healthier
one, because he controls d5 with pieces while d4 can never be covered by a white pawn. The side that
understands the knight manoeuvres wins these endings, and that is usually the King's Indian player. } *
